export const FR_C2_S07_ENGLISH: Record<string, string[]> = {
  "fr-c2-la-draille": [
    `Clémence Roux was thirty-one, with a veterinary doctorate from the Lyon school, a waterproof tablet and a precise mission: to fit GPS collars on the three thousand ewes of a pastoral association in the Crau during their transhumance to the high pastures of the Ubaye, and to show, with figures to back it up, that a connected flock got lost less often than an ordinary one. She had planned for everything except Baptiste Reboul.`,
    `The baïle, as the head shepherd is called in Provence, was seventy-two, wore a hat that was at least forty, and had a principled distrust of anything that needed recharging. "My animals recharge on grass, miss. And when they get lost, it's because some idiot drove them badly." He shook her hand all the same, in his own way, which consisted of looking at her for a long time without saying anything, then handing her a chestnut stick and saying: "Here. Your tablet won't help you on the stones."`,
    `They set off at night, on foot, as in the old days, because the association wanted part of the journey made along the drailles, the old transhumance tracks that have wound for centuries between farmhouses, vineyards and scrubland. At the head walked the menons, big castrated billy goats, majestic and bearded, wearing enormous bells round their necks. The flock followed them the way a river follows its bed. Clémence watched the three thousand little blue dots advancing in good order on her screen, and could not help feeling a rather childish pride.`,
    `For the first few days, technology triumphed. Thanks to the collars, Clémence spotted a lame ewe lagging at the back and another that had strayed into a lavender field, and she showed the curves to the baïle with a hint of triumph. Baptiste looked at the screen, nodded, then pointed with his chin at the lame ewe, which he had already noticed the day before, and at the lavender, where he knew she would go because she went there every year. "You've got a fine machine, little one. It sees what I see. But I also see what they're going to do."`,
    `On the fourth evening, as they were approaching the passes, the weather turned. A mountain storm, the kind that comes without warning and leaves without apologizing, fell on the flock right in the middle of a scree crossing. Hail crackled on the woolly backs. The lightning flashed so fast that the night turned white. Clémence, soaked to the bone, checked her tablet: no network, no satellite, nothing. Three thousand blue dots had gone out at once, and she realized with a kind of dread that without them she didn't know where three thousand very real animals were.`,
    `Baptiste wasn't looking at anything. He was listening. In the middle of the din he could make out, God knows how, the deep tone of the menons' bells as they advanced slowly, without panicking, towards a ledge they had known for years. "They know where to go," he said. "The ewes follow the menons, and the menons follow memory. Let them be." He whistled to his dogs and sent them to round up a few strays on the flanks, and the flock, as if magnetized by the sound of the bells, gathered of its own accord in the shelter of a rock wall. In the morning they counted: not a single animal was missing.`,
    `Clémence got her network back two days later, in the village of Jausiers. Her data showed, for the night of the storm, a nine-hour black hole, which her algorithm conscientiously interpreted as "total loss of the flock". She laughed for the first time since setting out, for a long time, sitting on a low wall, under the puzzled gaze of a menon chewing on a bramble. Baptiste sat down beside her and offered her a piece of tomme and a sip of wine cut with water, which, for him, was worth a diploma.`,
    `She wrote her report at the high pasture, under a larch. In it she defended the collars, sincerely: they made it possible to treat animals faster, to find stolen ones, to lighten the work of young shepherds who didn't have sixty years of draille in their legs. But she added one recommendation, which her manager in Lyon read twice before approving: never to fit collars on the menons. "Someone in the flock," she wrote, "has to go on knowing the way without being told."`,
  ],
  "fr-c2-la-proella": [
    `On Ouessant, the sea does not always give back the bodies. It keeps them, like a fussy creditor, and for centuries people have learned to do without them. When an island sailor disappeared at sea, a small wax cross was made, the proëlla, which stood in for the remains. It was laid on the table of the house, under a white cloth, between two candles; a vigil was kept all night, the prayers for the dead were said, then it was carried to the church, where it joined, in a wooden urn, those of the other sailors the ocean had refused to return.`,
    `In November 1927, Marie-Jeanne Malgorn kept vigil over her husband's. Yves had gone coasting on a Brest coaster that had sunk, they said, off the Scilly Isles, with all hands. The company had sent a letter, the rector had come, and the neighbours too, dressed in black, as the women of Ouessant always are when they have a dead man somewhere in the water. Marie-Jeanne did not cry. She looked all night at the little yellowish cross, so light, so derisory, and thought that Yves, who weighed eighty kilos and sang out of tune, would never have fitted into it whole.`,
    `The proëlla was carried to the church of Lampaul the next day. Marie-Jeanne went back to her life: the black sheep, the vegetable garden sheltered behind its dry-stone walls, the peat to dry, the pension to apply for. On the island, it has always been the women who run everything, the land, the money, the houses, and even marriage proposals, which they make themselves once they have chosen. She knew how to hold on. She grew used to the silence of the house, then to her own silence, with a speed that frightened her a little.`,
    `In March, on a drizzly morning, Yves Malgorn stepped off the Brest mail boat onto the slipway at Le Stiff. He was thin, bearded, wearing a Norwegian oilskin that was too big for him. His ship had indeed sunk, but a cargo ship from Bergen had fished him out half dead, clinging to a piece of wreckage, and landed him in Norway, where he had spent three months in hospital, without papers, without a common language, with a fever that had taken even his own name from him. He had written two letters. Neither had arrived.`,
    `The news crossed the island faster than the wind. When Yves pushed open the door of his house, Marie-Jeanne was standing by the fire, and she didn't move. They looked at each other for a long moment. He said, in Breton, the word one says on coming home from fishing, the everyday one. She answered in kind. Then she sat down, because her legs would no longer hold her, and at last she began to cry, four months of tears all at once, and he didn't know whether it was from joy or something else, and neither did she.`,
    `For there was, in this return, something nobody dared name. Yves had been mourned, buried, entered in the register. His cross was sleeping in the church urn. The old women on the path crossed themselves when they met him, not quite knowing whether they were greeting a living man or a ghost. And Marie-Jeanne herself, at night, watched him sleep with a tenderness shot through with dread, the way you look at someone you have already lost once, and whom you now know you can lose.`,
    `After a week she went to see the rector to ask him to give back the proëlla. One couldn't, she said, leave in the urn the cross of a man who ate his soup every evening at her table. The rector, a man from Landerneau who knew the island's customs poorly but the Church's very well, answered gently that what had been given to God could not be taken back. Marie-Jeanne thought for a long time, in the half-light of the sacristy, her hands folded on her black apron.`,
    `"Keep it, then, Father," she said at last. "That one is mine. It's the man I lost. The other one, the one who came back, I haven't quite found again yet. I'll take my time." She took off her mourning clothes that very evening, but kept her black shawl, out of habit, she said. Yves lived another thirty-one years. They say he never went back to sea, and that every Sunday, after mass, he went to greet his own cross, with a slightly embarrassed politeness, the way you greet a cousin you never really knew.`,
  ],
  "fr-c2-l-enseigne": [
    `When the Haeffelé bakery in the rue des Marchands in Colmar closed its doors after a hundred and seventy years, the last baker's granddaughter, Odile, a restorer of wall paintings by trade, obtained permission from the town to save the sign. It was a large painted wooden panel hung above the shop window, which read in gold letters on a green background: "Boulangerie – Pâtisserie J. Haeffelé". Everyone in Colmar had always seen it. Nobody had ever looked at it.`,
    `Odile set the panel on two trestles in her workshop in Little Venice and began, with scalpel and solvent, the patient work that in the trade is called a test window. Under the 1945 layer she expected to find grime and wood. She found letters. First, in black Gothic characters: "Bäckerei Josef Haeffelé". She recognized the date at once, or at least the era. In 1940, Alsace, annexed de facto by the Reich, had seen French banned even on berets, and first names had been Germanized by decree: Joseph had become Josef, by order.`,
    `She went on. Under the Bäckerei of 1940, a boulangerie of 1919, in fine, slightly naive tricolour capitals, with the first name back to Joseph and, in a corner, a little rooster painted by an enthusiastic hand. Under the boulangerie of 1919, another Bäckerei, plainer, older, in imperial brown letters, that of 1871, when Alsace had become German by the Treaty of Frankfurt. And under that, a boulangerie of 1860, under the Second Empire, in the French style, with scrolls and ears of wheat. Four times in seventy years, the family had had to repaint its own language.`,
    `Odile's grandmother, Marthe, ninety-four, came to see the sign at the workshop, leaning on her stick. She was born in 1931, French since Alsace was French then; then at nine she had been declared German, then at fourteen French again, without ever leaving the same street. She looked for a long time at the successive layers, the way you look at a family album where everyone wears a different costume. "My grandfather used to say we were the only bakers in Europe to change country without changing ovens," she said in Alsatian, and Odile, who understood it but spoke it badly, laughed with her.`,
    `Odile made the last test alone, one December evening, when the workshop smelled of turpentine and of the Christmas biscuits a neighbour had brought. Under the boulangerie of 1860, she no longer expected anything. The wood was bare, she thought. But the solvent brought out, barely legible, a few words traced by hand, without art, with a clumsy brush, probably by the founder himself in 1852, before anyone cared which country he belonged to: "Do gitt's Brot". Here, there is bread. In Alsatian.`,
    `She stayed in front of those three words for a long time. Neither the Empire, nor the Reich, nor the Republic had written them. They asserted nothing, claimed nothing, saluted no flag. They simply said what was behind the window, in the language of those who walked past. All the other layers had been painted over them, to make them say something else; none had erased them. Odile thought that perhaps this was what Alsace was: a very simple sentence, covered with grand declarations, which had outlived them all.`,
    `The town wanted a restoration "to its original state", which, for them, meant the 1945 boulangerie, the only one the people of Colmar knew. Odile proposed something else, and defended her plan with a stubbornness that surprised even the deputy mayor for heritage. She would not choose a layer. She would clear a stepped window across the sign, showing, from top to bottom, each era beside the others, like the strata of a cliff. The last step, bottom right, would show the founder's three words.`,
    `The sign is now on display at the Unterlinden Museum, a few streets from the closed shop. Visitors stop, read the labels, take photos. Schoolchildren go straight to the bottom right and ask what it means. Odile has got into the habit, when she passes, of answering them in Alsatian first, then in French, and sometimes in German when there are tourists from Freiburg. They all understand, or nearly. It is a sentence that never needed translating to be true.`,
  ],
  "fr-c2-la-cabane-sur-le-saguenay": [
    `Gabrielle Fontaine held the agrégation in modern literature, taught French at a lycée in the fifth arrondissement and had never been so cold in her life. It was minus thirty-two degrees on the Baie des Ha! Ha!, at La Baie, in the Saguenay, and her great-uncle Réal, eighty-one, had just explained to her, pulling a wool toque down to her eyebrows, that it was "a nice cold day, not bad at all".`,
    `Réal Tremblay was the brother of her grandmother, who had married a Frenchman in 1962 and had only ever regretted it in winter. He had lived alone since his wife's death, in a wooden house facing the fjord, and spent every cold season in his ice-fishing cabin, a little plywood box set on the ice, with a wood stove, two holes in the floor and a radio that picked up only the weather. That was where he had wanted to welcome Gabrielle. "Here, at least, we can chat in peace."`,
    `They chatted. Or rather, he chatted, and she corrected, at first in silence, then less and less so. She couldn't help it: it was her job. When he said he had "parked his car" (using "parker" and "char") near the shore, she pointed out that "parker" was an anglicism. When he spoke of his "blonde" (girlfriend) from long ago, she smiled knowingly. When he announced he was going to "checker les lignes", she sighed. Réal let her go on for a good while, poking at his stove, a mischievous glint under his bushy eyebrows.`,
    `"You know, my dear," he said at last, "I've got nothing against the French from France. But don't go thinking it's us who changed. When my ancestors left Normandy and the Perche, in sixteen-hundred-and-something, they said "icitte", they said "astheure", they said "frette" and "itou". It's you lot who stopped. We kept the words in the ice, like the fish. They keep well."`,
    `Gabrielle opened her mouth to answer, then closed it, because she knew he was partly right. She had read Rabelais and Montaigne; she had come across turns of phrase there that the old man used without thinking. She also knew that "char", which made her smile, was an old word for a carriage, which the Québécois had simply kept when the motor car arrived, and that it was, after all, a nice find. She fell silent for the first time that day, and Réal seemed to savour that silence more than the best catch.`,
    `The fish weren't biting. They ate cretons on toast, drank over-sweetened tea, listened to the ice cracking beneath them with that noise of giant crockery which, the first time, had made Gabrielle jump. Réal told her about the Oka crisis, the Saguenay flood of 1996, his wife Pierrette who laughed so loudly you could hear her from the other side of the bay. He spoke of his life the way you pay out a line, slowly, without pulling, letting the thread find its place. Gabrielle was taking notes without realizing it, in the notebook where she usually recorded her pupils' mistakes.`,
    `Towards the end of the afternoon, as the sun skimmed the fjord and tinted the snow pink, Réal pulled in the lines one by one, more carefully than was necessary. "This is my last season," he said without looking at her. "The ice sets later and later, and it goes out earlier and earlier. Last year they closed the fishing village two weeks ahead of time. I don't want to be the one they fish out in the spring. I'm selling the cabin to a young fellow from Chicoutimi. I wanted someone from the family to see it first."`,
    `Gabrielle could find nothing to say, which, for an agrégée, was an event. She looked at the little room, the stove, the black holes in the ice, the old man putting away his lines, and understood that she had not been invited to fish. On the way out, she pulled up her toque, breathed in the air so cold it burned, and said, in a voice that surprised even her: "It sure is frette, Uncle." Réal laughed for a long time, holding his sides. "Well, there you go. Now you're family."`,
  ],
  "fr-c2-la-parole-et-le-micro": [
    `Awa Diallo was twenty-eight, with a very good digital recorder and a conviction: the memory of West Africa was dying, old man by old man, and it had to be saved before it was too late. She worked for a community radio station in Saint-Louis, the former colonial capital with its houses with faded balconies, between the river and the ocean. She had obtained a small grant to record the griots of the region. The first on her list was El Hadji Samba Mbaye, eighty-seven, reputed to know the history of all the great families of the Walo.`,
    `He received her in the courtyard of his house, on the island, in the shade of a mango tree, with the teranga owed to every visitor: mint tea, poured from high up, in three rounds, the first bitter like life, the second mild like love, the third sweet like death, as the saying goes. Awa explained her project enthusiastically. She talked about servers, backups, transmission to future generations. She quoted the famous line about the old man who dies and the library that burns. The griot listened to the end without interrupting her, then laughed softly.`,
    `"My daughter," he said, "you want to put my words in your little box. But words you lock up can no longer answer. When I tell the history of the Walo, I never tell it the same way twice. I look at who is listening. If there's a descendant of the Braks, I dwell on their courage; if there's a child who's bored, I make the horses gallop. Your box will always speak the same way, to anybody, like a parrot that has lost its master. You won't save my memory. You'll save its corpse."`,
    `Awa did not lose heart. She came back the next day, then the day after, with arguments and sugar. She pointed out that the young people of Saint-Louis no longer came to sit in courtyards, that they listened to podcasts, that the griots themselves now sang at weddings for politicians and wealthy emigrants, and that a dead memory was better than no memory at all. The old man listened, nodded, served the tea. "You speak well," he would say. "You have the tongue of a gewel. A pity you have the ears of an archivist."`,
    `After a week he made her an offer. He agreed to be recorded, on one condition: he would not be the one telling. She would. He would first tell her the story, once, without a microphone; then she would have to tell it in turn, in front of him, in her own words, and he would correct her at every mistake. That, and only that, was what she would record. "Memory isn't something you keep, it's something you pass on. Nit nitay garabam: man is the remedy of man. If you want my story to live, it has to go through you."`,
    `It was the hardest trial of her career. Awa, who spoke on air every day with ease, found herself stammering, hesitant, unable to remember the genealogies, mixing up the kings and the battles. The griot corrected her without mercy, sometimes with irritation, often with a humour that mortified her. But as the weeks went by, the names began to hold together, the horses to gallop, and she noticed one day that she was telling the story of Queen Ndatté Yalla while watching her audience, as he had taught her, and changing her tone when a child yawned.`,
    `The programme was broadcast during the rainy season, in ten episodes. In it you hear Awa's voice, young, sometimes unsure, telling the history of the Walo. And behind her, now and then, you hear another voice, deeper, that interrupts her, corrects her, adds a detail, gently mocks a mispronunciation. Awa cut nothing in the edit. Listeners wrote in by the hundreds; many asked who this old man was who meddled in everything. Others, even more numerous, asked whether they could come and sit in the courtyard.`,
    `El Hadji Samba Mbaye died the following year, at the end of the rainy season. Awa did what was expected of her: she put the files on three different servers, with backups. But she also now tells the history of the Walo in the neighbourhood schools, without a microphone, once a month. She never tells it the same way twice. And she has got into the habit, when a child interrupts her to correct her, of saying jërëjëf, thank you, with a smile that no one in the class can quite understand.`,
  ],
};

